 okay what was the last  time at at which a data data was  committed for this key and with that  information it can serve the data from  locally to the to the user now the  obvious question here is what happens if  the data hasn't been sent in this case  the mutation hasn't been sent to the non  leader splits which is a great question  what happens is as I said earlier as  part of the commit we're actually  sending out the mutations to all the  other replicas so chances are that it's  already been received by you know split  to zone a in this case and will be  served by the zone a split to to the  requester if it is the case that you  know the zone a split to hasn't received  data at time T bang as is mentioned here  which is the latest timestamp of commit  then there might be a little bit of a  wait to get to that timestamp and then  get the data that times them and then  respond back but as I said in most cases  because we're sending the data out  parallely there shouldn't be  these latencies are generally hidden  within the commit time itself in terms  of a boundedly still read as I talked  about earlier if you make a request for  this key one two three again but with a  maximum 15 second staleness  what that means is we the local replica  is confident that it has the data at  that time the the older you go the more  chances are that this you know the the  mutations have been replicated to the  other  replicas which means that it can serve  this request without even having to talk  to the leader so in terms of single role  rights so basically what this means is  that we're trying to commit data to a  single key or single row in this  configuration in this case it's a three  zone configuration but you know the same  applies to a Multi multi reading  configuration as well so basically the  requester makes a write request and then  the write request goes directly to the  leader and again the API and the and the  system itself know where the leader is  so the write request is directly routed  to the leader the leader acquires locks  that's you know the whole point of  leadership is to to be the sole  proprietor of committing data to those  committing changes to that data and  therefore it has the lock so it'll  acquire their locks and then it will  send the write request to the other  replicas within the quorum in this case  the two other replicas in this  configuration and the replicas will  basically act so it's basically  two-phase commit for the replicas will  accept they're ready to write that they  agree with this proposal and once the  the the split leaders but to leader in  Zone B gets acts back from a majority so  basically from one of the zone in this  case right  two out of three or three out of five  it'll release the locks and it'll apply  the changes locally and then send their  success request back to the to the  requester right so again as I was saying  earlier as part of the commit protocol  the rights are actually mutations are  actually being sent out which means that  you don't actually have to wait for the  data to be applied at the leader and  then have it the data be replicated to  other replicas the mutations are being  replicated as part of the commit and  therefore they're being applied  independently and chances are that if  you read the same data after the commit  response has come back that you will get  the data immediately from the local  replicas  let's look at a more interesting case  which is writing across two different  splits so if you want to write across in  a table or multiple tables which happen  to be on two different splits then those  splits will have to coordinate in order  to commit the right so let's look an  example where we are going to commit  data such that split one and split two  iron bolt as you can see from this  diagram split one is as leader in zones  C and split two has leader in Zone B so  when a write request comes in to commit  data to row x and y as part of that  write request the coordinator is already  chosen by the client which means chosen  by the API itself in this case we have  chosen Zone C leader to be the  coordinator so each of these splits will  acquire the locks for making changes to  row x and y and then they will  independently talk to their replica  across the multiple zones right so at  this point similar to the previous  single row case basically they replicate  the data across the two other zones for  their split and then once they have  received acknowledgments back from those  two other replicated split groups from  their group they will respond back to  the coordinator which is the split one  in zone C to say that they're ready to  commit at that point because split C is  the coordinator it'll basically tell  split two to do the commit of the data  at which point each group leader will  then tell its other replicas to apply  the change and the success is returned  back to the client so in this case the  same protocol that we were talking about  in the single row case happens at each  split level where each split leader  informs its other  does to you know apply the change and to  make the proposal for the change  but then there's coordination among  split leaders on doing the commit for  all the roads that are part of this  commit so I know we covered a lot of  material but hopefully it gave you a  sneak peek at or at least a better  understanding at how cloud spanner works  under the covers to give get external  global consistency stay tuned for a live  Q&amp;A we'll be back in less than a minute  you  welcome back we have a few questions  that we can go over in with regard to  what we just discussed so let's get to  them so first question how does spanner  know which kind of read the client is  asking for and should this functionality  be built into the client so yes the  question was answered basically the  functionality is actually built into the  API where the API knows where to route  the read also the the API itself when  you make a request you can actually when  you are specifying bounded staleness  it's it's part of the call part of the  request that you make is to say that you  know I can tolerate bounded stainless up  to let's say 15 seconds or 10 minutes or  whatever and because of that like it is  a pretty big hint to the API that it  should use the bounded stale read  request instead of a strong read if you  specify nothing and just say give me key  1 value 1 then by default we give you  the strong reads right because we expect  like all databases relational database  to read we expect you to want the the  consistent read answer the most freshest  read that just happened hopefully the  answer is that any plans to have a  standalone simulator to test spanner yes  we have a plan for that basically we're  working on open sourcing our sequel  engine and the the relevant parts of it  that will then allow us to build a  simulator we used locally not on  machines and stuff and you don't have to  constantly be connected to the cloud and  machines and stuff and you don't have to  constantly be connected to the cloud  we're working on it and you know  hopefully we'll have it in soon enough  next question the spanner support column  constraints such as foreign key  constraints so so far I've been support  foreign key constraints in the form of  interleaves tables so basically if you  interleave a table into a parent then  that automatically gives it a foreign  key constraint where you have to have  the parent in order to have the child  well beyond that we don't  have foreign key constraints in the in  the product yet hopefully will we have  it on our back lot and hopefully we'll  have it sometime in the future so that's  all the time we had for questions today  hopefully you enjoyed the talk and stay  tuned for talks coming every Tuesday  from cloud on air webinars from Google  cloud thank you and have a good day
 

